::: {.doc-subject}

Find shortest path using Bellman-Ford algorithm.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L67187-L67396){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.bellman_ford_shortest_path(
    source,
    target,
    attr_name=None,
    return_list=False,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Bellman-Ford shortest path](../assets/graphs/bellman-ford-shortest-path.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A directed weighted graph with three routes from A to F, one of whose edges, D to F, has weight minus 2. Bellman-Ford handles the negative weight, so the returned path A, D, F is in the accent color. The card reads cost 3 + (−2) = 1." width="100%"}

Computes the shortest weighted path from source to target using the Bellman-Ford algorithm.
Unlike Dijkstra's algorithm, Bellman-Ford can handle negative edge weights and will detect
negative weight cycles.

Can return the path as a list, or store the position of each node along the path as a node
attribute for visualization.

The Bellman-Ford algorithm is slower than Dijkstra (O(VE) vs O(E log V)) but more versatile.
Use it when:

- the graph has negative edge weights
- the graph is sparse, with few edges
- you need to detect negative cycles

Edge weights are always used, whether or not the graph was created with
`weighted=True`; edges without a weight count as `1.0`. In directed graphs, the path
follows edge directions. In an undirected graph each edge can be traversed both ways, so
a single negative edge that is reachable from `source=` already forms a negative cycle
and raises an error; negative weights are therefore only meaningful in directed graphs.
Only cycles reachable from `source=` are detected; a negative cycle elsewhere in the
graph does not affect the result. If several paths have the same total weight, one of
them is returned.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Node where the path starts, given as an integer node ID or a string label. An ID or
    label that isn't in the graph raises a `NodeNotFoundError` (a `ValueError`).

<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Node where the path ends, given as an integer node ID or a string label, with the
    same rules as `source=`. If `target=` equals `source=`, the path is just that node.

<code>[attr_name]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Name of the node attribute to store path position. If `None`, uses
    `bellman_ford_path_order` as the attribute name. Position is 0-indexed (source = 0,
    next node = 1, ..., target = n-1). Only nodes on the path get this attribute set;
    all other nodes get a null value. Only used when `return_list=` is `False`.

<code>[return_list]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True`, returns list of node IDs in the path and leaves the node DataFrame
    untouched. If `False` (default), stores positions as node attributes under
    `attr_name=` and returns self for chaining.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Union[list[int], Graph, None]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `return_list=` is `True`: list of node IDs from source to target (inclusive),
    or `None` if no path exists.
    If `return_list=` is `False`: returns self for method chaining, or `None` if no path
    exists.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph contains a negative weight cycle reachable from `source=`, or if
    `source=` or `target=` is an ID or label that does not exist in the graph.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

Bellman-Ford relaxes every edge $V - 1$ times, so it costs $O(V \cdot E)$ time and
$O(V)$ space, one factor slower than Dijkstra's $O((V + E) \log V)$. What justifies
the extra work is that Bellman-Ford tolerates *negative* edge weights: whenever a
shorter path exists that dips below the current distance estimate, the next
relaxation round finds it. A final $V$-th pass tries to relax again, and any
distance that still decreases proves the existence of a negative-weight cycle, which
the implementation surfaces as `ValueError` rather than returning an unbounded
path. That negative-cycle detector is what makes Bellman-Ford the standard tool for
arbitrage detection in currency graphs, cost-penalty routing where discounts appear
as negative weights, and game-theoretic setups with negative payoffs, on top of the
plain shortest-path role it plays on graphs with occasional negative edges.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~shortest_path`) 

:   Dijkstra's algorithm, faster when no weight is negative.

[](`~find_negative_cycle`) 

:   The cycle that makes shortest paths undefined.

[](`~goldberg_radzik`) 

:   Shortest paths from a source to every node, with negative weights.

[](`~johnson`) 

:   Shortest paths between all pairs, with negative weights.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

The direct edge from A to B costs `4`, but going through C costs `2 + (-3) = -1`, so
the cheapest route to D detours through C and then takes the negative edge to B:

::: {#6c584ab9 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B", 4.0), ("A", "C", 2.0), ("C", "B", -3.0), ("B", "D", 1.0)],
    directed=True,
)

path = graph.bellman_ford_shortest_path("A", "D", return_list=True)
[graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in path]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['A', 'C', 'B', 'D']
```
:::
:::


With `return_list=False` (the default), each node on the path gets its position along it
in the `bellman_ford_path_order` node attribute, and the other nodes get a null value.
`highlight=` can pick the path out by that attribute:

::: {#0955378e .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
spots = {"A": (0, 1), "B": (2, 1), "C": (1, 0), "D": (4, 1)}
pos = {graph.get_id_from_label(v): p for v, p in spots.items()}
graph.set_node_attrs("pos", pos)

graph.bellman_ford_shortest_path("A", "D").plot(
    layout="pos", highlight="bellman_ford_path_order",
    edge_label_by="weight", label_by="label", height=240,
)
```

